  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)-2x \\\\  & =2x(4-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x) \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =2(4-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)-2x\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =8-4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x-4 \\\\  & =4(1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x) & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061x & <1 \\\\ x & <e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on  $(0,e).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $4(1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)<0,$ which has the solution $(e,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
